+# Models, agents, effort and resource bounds
 
-These are configurable starting assignments, not comparative benchmark findings. Check actual account access and installed CLI support before using a model ID. An unavailable model blocks the job; do not silently spend on another provider.
+From 0.3.4 every agent role is a **pinned model id plus an explicit effort**, run with
+`--strict-routing`. These are configurable starting assignments, not benchmark findings: measure
+them on your own completed tickets (see "Evaluating the routing" below) before changing them.
 
-| Agent / job | Starting model | Responsibility |
+## Roles
+
+| Role | Where it runs | Default model / effort | Responsibility |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Coordinator | Your desktop session (not launched by the factory) | Opus 5.5 / medium | Interview, specification, tickets, allocation, escalation, final report |
+| Architecture supervisor | Your desktop session, on request | Fable 5.2 / high | ADRs, cross-cutting design decisions, audits of the factory itself |
+| Read-only classification / summary | Your desktop session | Sonnet 5.5 / low | Status summaries, ticket triage; never writes code |
+| `builder_routine` | Factory | `claude-sonnet-5-5` / medium | One scoped, ordinary ticket (`complexity: routine`, the default) |
+| `builder_complex` | Factory | `claude-opus-5-5` / high | Ticket marked `complexity: complex` (cross-module, tricky state, migrations) |
+| `builder_critical` | Factory | `claude-opus-5-5` / high | Ticket with `role: critical` (authz, integrity, money, privacy) |
+| `reviewer_standards` | Factory, fresh profile | `claude-opus-5-5` / high | Code standards, defects, security relevant to the diff |
+| `reviewer_spec` | Factory, fresh profile | `claude-opus-5-5` / high | Acceptance criteria, scope, missed behaviour |
+| `reviewer_standards_critical` | Factory, `role: critical` only | `claude-opus-5-5` / high | As above, for critical tickets |
+| `reviewer_spec_critical` | Factory, `role: critical` only | `claude-fable-5-2` / high | Exceptional-risk escalation: a second model family reviews critical work |
+
+Routing is by **risk and complexity**, not by frontend/backend: which suites run is decided by the
+ticket's `paths` (verification policy), which builder runs is decided by `role: critical` and
+`complexity`. Configurations without `builder_*` keys keep routing by `role` (pre-0.3.4 behaviour).
+Fable is used only for critical-risk escalation unless you select it explicitly with `--route`;
+it is not used for polling, summaries or ordinary coding.
+
+Codex stays a supported provider for any role: `configure.py --route builder_routine=codex:MODEL:medium`.
+Codex accepts `low`, `medium`, `high` (passed as `-c model_reasoning_effort=...`). Codex reports
+no per-run model usage to the bridge, so its evidence records `served_models_verified: false`.
+
+## What `--strict-routing` guarantees (Claude CLI 2.1.322, verified on the host)
+
+| Risk found | Behaviour of the CLI | What the bridge does |
 |---|---|---|
-| Coordinator in Claude Code Desktop | `opus` | Interview, specification, ticket dependencies, allocation, escalation, final report |
-| Alternative coordinator in Codex Desktop | `gpt-6.1-sol` | Same responsibility; only one coordinator owns a repository run |
-| Frontend / ordinary UI builder | Claude `sonnet` | One scoped ticket and its behavior tests |
-| Backend / API / data builder | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` | One scoped ticket and its behavior tests |
-| Critical authorization / integrity work | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol`, higher reasoning if locally supported | Explicit negative/race/recovery tests; no cheap-model downgrade |
-| Standards reviewer | Fresh Codex `gpt-6.1-sol` | Code standards, defects, security/integrity relevant to the diff |
-| Specification reviewer | Fresh Claude `opus` | Original acceptance criteria, scope and missed behavior |
+| Effort typo | `--effort ultra` is accepted silently (exit 0) | Effort must be one of `low medium high xhigh max` (`low medium high` for Codex); anything else is refused before the CLI starts |
+| Environment overrides the flag | `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` "overrides effort for this session", even over `--effort` | Sets `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` to the requested effort **and** passes `--effort`, so both agree |
+| Settings cap clamps silently | `maxEffortLevel` in any settings file (lowest wins) clamps `--effort` without reporting it | Reads managed, user and project settings; a cap below the request refuses the run |
+| Alias remapping | `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_{OPUS,SONNET,HAIKU,FABLE}_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` can change what an alias means | Requires full model ids (`claude-opus-5-5`, not `opus`) and removes those variables for the run |
+| Silent fallback | `--fallback-model` / overload fallback can switch models | Never passes `--fallback-model`; sets `CLAUDE_CODE_NO_MODEL_FALLBACK=1`; refuses the result unless `modelUsage` shows the requested model served the run |
+| Unknown or unavailable model | `is_error: true`, exit 1 | The run fails closed (the ticket blocks); evidence records `cli_failed` |
+
+Not observable: the CLI does not report the *effective* effort of a run (not in the result
+envelope, the init message or the debug log). The bridge therefore controls every input it can
+(flag, environment, caps) and records them; it cannot prove what the server applied. An
+organisation-level per-model effort cap set on the account is invisible locally.
+
+## Evidence per agent run
+
+Next to each result file the bridge writes `<result>.routing.json` (mode 0600, exclusive create,
+stale files refused): provider, mode, requested model and effort, the effort controls applied, the
+overrides removed, settings caps found, models that served the run with token counts and cost,
+duration, turns, the names of tools whose use was denied (never their inputs), exit status and an
+outcome (`ok`, `cli_failed`, `routing_refused`, `error`). It never contains credentials or
+environment values. Builders also keep their full `stream-json` transcript
+(`builder-result.transcript.jsonl`). The factory records the builder's routing in the ledger and
+attaches each reviewer's to its review; for a strict command, missing or unsuccessful routing
+evidence blocks the ticket.
+
+## Evaluating the routing
 
-Use one durable coordinator, up to two builders, and fresh reviewer processes. Review sessions are short-lived roles, not permanently running agents. A process semaphore caps active CLI subprocesses at two. Two builders can overlap; when review slots are available the coordinator dispatches the two lenses. Workers and reviewers do not allocate further agents. This permits two tickets without an unbounded agent tree.
+After every completed ticket, keep per role/model/effort: outcome (READY first time, attempts used,
+BLOCKED reason), review findings by severity, cold-suite failures caught after the builder, tokens
+and cost from the routing evidence, and wall-clock time. Change a default only when a few tickets
+of the same complexity show a consistent difference (for example routine tickets that keep
+blocking on Sonnet/medium, or complex tickets that pass first time on Sonnet). Record the change and
+its evidence in the project's decision log.
 
-The external desktop coordinator is additional to the two-process runner budget. Model costs and subscription usage depend on the actual host/account; this package neither measures token cost nor promises a dollar cap. `timeout_seconds` bounds each process, not total project cost.
+## Resource bounds
 
-## Evidence for role selection
+Use one durable coordinator, up to two builders, and fresh reviewer processes. A process semaphore
+caps active CLI subprocesses at two. Workers and reviewers do not allocate further agents.
+`timeout_seconds` bounds each process, not total project cost.
 
